{"version":2,"text":"Vitamin C Deficiency: Texts\n\nText D\n\nVITAMIN C EXCESS AND TOXICITY\n\nHigh doses of vitamin C are usually not toxic to healthy adults. Occasionally, higher doses\ncause nausea or diarrhoea and interfere with the interpretation of some blood test results.\n\nSome people take high doses of vitamin C because it is an antioxidant, which protects cells\nagainst damage by free radicals. Free radicals are thought to contribute to many disorders,\nsuch as atherosclerosis, cancer, lung disorders, the common cold, eye cataracts, and\nmemory loss. Whether taking high doses of vitamin C protects against or has any beneficial\neffect on these disorders is unclear. Evidence of a protective effect against cataracts is\nstrongest.\n\nPage 409\n\nEmail: info@oetonline.net.au","layout":{"version":1,"width":595.276001,"height":841.890015,"blocks":[{"text":"Vitamin C Deficiency: Texts","lines":[{"text":"Vitamin C Deficiency: Texts","x":0.3501387568957278,"y":0.15742071842959204,"width":0.3360589368023254,"height":0.02569931893063252,"confidence":1,"words":[{"text":"Vitamin","x":0.3501387568957278,"width":0.09712472013465225,"confidence":1},{"text":"C","x":0.45367394880076817,"width":0.01753808314540132,"confidence":1},{"text":"Deficiency:","x":0.4776225037165575,"width":0.1390909222963955,"confidence":1},{"text":"Texts","x":0.6218842039291284,"width":0.06431348976892481,"confidence":1}]}]},{"text":"Text D","lines":[{"text":"Text D","x":0.06300909315509261,"y":0.2070957000244266,"width":0.06948076678804324,"height":0.022843839049451132,"confidence":1,"words":[{"text":"Text","x":0.06300909315509261,"width":0.04609859116426902,"confidence":1},{"text":"D","x":0.11475349566461021,"width":0.017736364278525648,"confidence":1}]}]},{"text":"VITAMIN C EXCESS AND TOXICITY","lines":[{"text":"VITAMIN C EXCESS AND TOXICITY","x":0.05816797576558105,"y":0.2597872074774518,"width":0.3238477406718098,"height":0.019989116986973664,"confidence":1,"words":[{"text":"VITAMIN","x":0.0